      "status_as_recorded": "inconclusive",
      "finding": "Ranked playsheet validation (counter ranking of BEST vs AVOID after defensive H2 suppression) was inconclusive due to low measurement reliability, small sample, and leakage risks."
    }
  ],
  "failed_approaches": [
    {
      "id": "FAM:SF-APPS-085",
      "what_failed": "Split-half reliability test of a half-based player trait (connector quality Q_p)",
      "why": "Measurement reliability too low; the question was rated untestable (A_unanswerable) because the trait variance within-player overwhelmed between-player signal."
    },
    {
      "id": "FAM:SF-APPS-018",
      "what_failed": "Validation of defensive suppression triggering effective counter plays based on H2-H1 drop",
      "why": "Inconclusive results, limited by low measurement reliability, small sample, and leakage/stationarity risks."
    }
  ],
  "evidence_establishes": [
    "Split-half (H1 vs H2) measurements of player-level traits can be overwhelmingly noisy, making such traits untestable for year-to-year or within-season stability without large samples.",
    "Some split-half reliability analyses of other metrics (e.g., structural influence residuals) have successfully demonstrated stability, so not all half-based metrics are inherently untestable.",
    "No study has directly examined whether a defense's effect on opponents' H2-H1 change is a reliable team trait."
  ],
  "evidence_does_not_establish": [
    "Whether aggregating H2-H1 differentials at the team-season level across many games (82 per season) yields sufficient measurement reliability to detect a stable trait.",
    "Whether year-to-year correlations of team-level defensive halftime adjustments are practically large enough to be detected with the available sample of ~30 team pairs per consecutive season.",
    "The degree to which confounding by opponent quality, game script, coach/roster changes, and regression to the mean would distort estimates."
  ],
  "own_inferences": [
    "The direct precedent (SF-APPS-085) suggests that half-split traits can be too noisy to replicate even at modest sample sizes; if the true team-level signal is similarly weak, the question will be untestable with only 3-4 seasons of data.",
    "Within-season split-half reliability (e.g., splitting a team's 82 games into two halves) would provide more data points (120 team-seasons) and could be used as an intermediate test, but it would measure internal consistency rather than the trait's stability across seasons and roster changes, which is typically implied by 'reliable team trait'.",
    "Even with careful covariate adjustment (opponent offensive rating, home court, game script), the residual may retain substantial noise, and the number of independent team-season observations likely limits the power to detect a correlation smaller than ≈0.35."
  ],
  "uncertainties": [
    "The true magnitude of year-to-year correlation of the defensive H2-H1 effect is unknown; it could be as high as 0.5 if coaching staffs consistently excel at halftime adjustments, or near zero.",
    "The optimal operationalization (e.g., adjusting for opponent pre-game offensive efficiency, using weighted averages, excluding garbage time) remains undefined and could substantially alter reliability.",
    "Coaching changes, player turnover, and strategic evolution across seasons may weaken any persistent signal beyond what measurement noise already does.",
    "The available 2025-26 season is partial, complicating cross-season analyses and possibly introducing selection effects."
  ],
  "historical_effect": {
    "changed_decision": "yes",
    "explanation": "Without the historical evidence, I might have assumed that a team-level aggregate metric over an 82-game season would be sufficiently stable to test year-to-year reliability. The retrieval of SF-APPS-085, which found a similar half-based trait untestable due to low split-half reliability, directly warns that such questions often fail because the within-variation dwarfs any between-trait signal. This shifted my assessment from a tentative 'proceed with careful power analysis' to 'do not proceed,' as the a priori likelihood of an adequately powered test appears low given the available sample and measurement noise."
